// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/trinet/e3_rx_capture_model_ax7203.t27 -- pre-registration: the E3 receive capture timing at
; the FPGA's RX flip-flops, in nextpnr-xilinx's own delay model, for the routed e3z netlist
; The one source for this check. conformance/e3_rx_capture_from_spec.mjs compiles this file with the
; t27 compiler (wasm), evaluates every test block below, checks the pinned repo files, reads back the
; constants it repeats from the E3 spec, the TX model's spec and log, the RTL and the XDC, and writes
; conformance/e3_rx_capture_params.py, which the runner conformance/e3_rx_capture_model.py reads.
; `--check` fails on drift. Written 2026-09-28T00:08:34Z (date -u), before the runner existed and
; before any RX delay was read out of the SDF: its layout was read with every digit masked. ASCII,
; English.

; WHY. E3 captures RGMII in fabric flip-flops, because IDDR does not capture through the open flow
; (openXC7/nextpnr#22). The RTL header says the falling edge of RXC is mid-nibble, "about +-20 ns of
; margin whatever that delay really is", and that a rising-edge sample "would have 1-2 ns and no
; constraint in this flow to hold it"; the `ed` counter compares the two on the board. RGMII puts
; RX_DV on RX_CTL for the rising edge and RX_DV xor RX_ER for the falling one, so the falling-edge
; RX_CTL sample, which E3 relies on to end a frame at an RX_ER (plan, liteeth#232 note), sits at
; that change, not mid-nibble: it has the rising-edge sample's 1-2 ns, not the +-20 ns. The TX model
; (e3_tx_hold_model_ax7203.t27, PASS) left an SDF of the routed e3z whose FASM equals the flashed
; build's. The RX side of the same file has not been read.

; WHAT RUNS. No tool. The runner reads SDF_FILE, ROUTED_FILE and FASM_FILE, all written by the TX
; model's one run (its log records the SDF's sha prefix and size and "fasm ... = e3z"), and refuses
; unless all three and the TX runner it imports match their pinned sha256. It checks the structure:
; each RXD pad's input buffer drives the D pin of its rxd_n and rxd_p1 flip-flops directly, the
; RX_CTL buffer drives rxctl_n directly, all nine flip-flops are clocked from the BUFGCTRL that drives
; RXC_NET, that buffer's I0 comes from the RXC pad's input buffer, and exactly rxd_n and rxctl_n are
; on the inverted clock. For each flip-flop f:
;   C_f   = net(RXC buffer -> BUFG.I0) + BUFG(I0 -> O) + net(BUFG.O -> f.CK)
;   D_f   = net(data pad's buffer -> f.D)
;   Delta = C_f - D_f
;   Tsu, Th = the largest setup and hold of f's (posedge D) and (negedge D) SETUPHOLD checks.
; The input buffers carry no delay in this SDF; the six are the same cell with the same IOSTANDARD
; and are taken to cancel (an assumption, not measured).

; THE PHY SIDE, fixed before the numbers. At the FPGA pads a data change lies x ps from the RXC edge
; it belongs to: x = s - P. P is the KSZ9031's RX clock delay at its reset pad skews, nominal
; PHY_DELAY_NOM_PS (Linux micrel.c: "internal RGMII_IDRX = 1.2ns"), taken as PHY_DELAY_LO_PS to
; PHY_DELAY_HI_PS. s is the board's RXC-to-data skew plus the PHY's output skew, within +-SKEW_PS
; (RGMII v2.0 TskewT). So x runs from -X_EARLY_MAX_PS (data before the edge) to +X_LATE_MAX_PS. The
; RTL header says the PHY changes RXD only near the rising edge at 100M; that is assumed here too.

; THE CHECK, the mid-nibble samples: rxd_n[0..3], and rxctl_n for nibbles without RX_ER.
;   setup = RXC_HIGH_MIN_PS + Delta - Tsu - X_LATE_MAX_PS     must be >= MARGIN_MIN_PS
;   hold  = RXC_LOW_MIN_PS - X_EARLY_MAX_PS - Delta - Th      must be >= MARGIN_MIN_PS
; (the falling edge at its earliest and latest within RGMII's 40-60 % duty cycle, the data change at
; its latest and earliest). MARGIN_MIN_PS is an allowance for what the model leaves out, chosen here.
;   PASS       all five meet both.
;   FAIL       one misses: the model says the +-20 ns claim does not hold for that pin.
;   NO VERDICT a pinned file differs, the structure is not the one above, or a flip-flop has no D
;              SETUPHOLD. Nothing is concluded then.

; INFORMATION, printed and not judged. A sample taken at a change (rxd_p1 at the rising edge; rxctl_n
; at the falling edge of an RX_ER nibble) takes the new value if Delta - Tsu >= x, the old one if
; Delta + Th <= x, and is undetermined in between. Over the whole x range:
;   rxd_p1[i]  new on all four -> `ed` stays 0 on the board; old on any -> `ed` counts; else
;              undetermined, and the board's `ed` then places x against the model's window.
;   rxctl_n    new -> RX_DV xor RX_ER is seen and an RX_ER ends the frame, as the plan assumes;
;              old -> RX_DV is seen and RX_ER never reaches the frame logic; else undetermined.
; The same is printed at the nominal x = -PHY_DELAY_NOM_PS.

; PREDICTION. PASS. Guesses, information only: `ed` and RX_ER undetermined over the whole range, and
; both on the "new" side at the nominal point.

; WHAT IT DOES NOT SAY. Anything measured: one corner, one number per arc, no clock jitter, no input
; buffer or pad delay, no board traces; P and s are assumed ranges, not the PHY's datasheet. The
; KSZ9031's RX pad-skew registers stay at reset values in E3. A PASS says that in the tool's own
; numbers the falling-edge capture keeps the margin the RTL comment claims; the information lines
; say which way the edge-sitting samples fall if the assumed ranges hold. It moves nothing in the E3
; spec and changes no E3 verdict; the board's `ed` is the test of the information lines.

